WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.836
-Our next guest
is a fantastic comedian

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:04.303
whose new book,
"You're Doing Great!

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:06.272
And Other Reasons to Stay
Alive," is available now.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:07.907
He's also the host
of the podcast

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.742
"Breaking Bread with Tom Papa."

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:11.811
Please welcome to the show
Tom Papa.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:14.981
Welcome to the show, Tom,
and I do want to start by saying

00:00:14.981 --> 00:00:17.216
that your book is in
the catbird seat here.

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:20.086
I'm displaying it with my copy
of "The Thorn Birds."

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:23.556
-Oh, I'm so -- I can't
tell you how honored I am.

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:25.725
Because I have in my --

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.094
When I was growing up,
we had "The Thorn Birds."

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:30.463
I think it was also
with "Lonesome Dove."

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:33.265
-Oh, that's a good one.
"Lonesome Dove" is a good one.

00:00:33.265 --> 00:00:38.471
-It's a good one. And to see my
book on that pile, I can't --

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:40.773
I can't lie, I've been
waiting for this moment.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:42.308
-We're happy to have you here.

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.177
And I want to talk
about the book.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:46.545
But we saw each other last year.

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:49.782
You are an incredibly
accomplished, talented comedian.

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:53.619
But I saw you give
an absolutely perfect --

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:56.622
pitch-perfect performance
as a pastor.

00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:59.625
You married Colin Quinn
and Jen Sochko,

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:01.660
who is a producer on our show.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.997
And you have actually married
some other comedians.

00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:05.498
Is this correct?

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:08.467
-Yeah, Colin was actually
my fifth one that I've done.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:10.170
I did Rachel Feinstein's

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.204
and Noam Dworman,
who owns the Comedy Cellar,

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:14.040
and Lenny Marcus.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:19.011
I became, like,
the go-to guy for weddings.

00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:22.214
-I tracked you down that night
to compliment the job you did,

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:24.884
because, you know, I think doing
comedy in front of comedians

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.820
is hard enough as it is.
-Yeah.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.524
-But to do it on a night that
everyone is going to remember.

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:34.327
Do you --
Versus regular joke-writing,

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:38.297
do you feel that pressure when
you're writing...like that?

00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:40.199
-Ooh. [ Chuckles ] Yeah.

00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:43.936
I mean, I wrote that thing
for six months straight.

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:46.672
I just kept writing
and writing and writing.

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:48.942
And especially knowing
that it's Colin Quinn,

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:51.243
who is one of the most
prolific, funniest,

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:53.779
and judgmental comedians
we have...

00:01:53.779 --> 00:01:56.349
standing this far from my face,

00:01:56.349 --> 00:02:00.052
I was -- Oh, the pressure,
every word, every line.

00:02:00.052 --> 00:02:01.988
And you were really funny
at the end of it

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:05.658
because you pointed out the
worst part of the whole thing

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:09.061
is, even if it goes well,
which it did, thank God --

00:02:09.061 --> 00:02:11.497
that's the only time
you get to do it.

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:12.965
-Yeah.
-And you said you've given

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:14.834
some commencement speeches
and things like that

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.403
and you've worked so hard,
and then, poof, it's just gone.

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:19.505
-Yup. You wrote this book,

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:22.775
and you obviously put
a lot of work into this.

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.777
And then it's called
"You're Doing Great!

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.378
And Other Reasons
to Stay Alive."

00:02:26.378 --> 00:02:29.582
But obviously this was written
before a pandemic hit

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:32.952
and basically came out
as this was happening.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:36.722
Were you -- Because this is
a book that is optimistic.

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:40.026
Were you worried that
optimistic message right now

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.928
would not be
what people would want?

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:44.897
And what have you found
the outcome to be?

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:48.234
-Yeah, you know, the line "And
Other Reasons to Stay Alive,"

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.469
I thought it was just
being funny.

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:51.770
[ Laughs ] You know?

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.773
I didn't know it was
going to be that relevant.

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:57.610
But when the first, like,
two weeks of the shutdown,

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.679
the shelter at home,
I had to go do the audio book.

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:03.449
So I drove in L.A. --

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:06.452
only car on the road,
very secretive --

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:08.821
went to, like,
an undisclosed location.

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.457
One guy with a mask.
And I was conscious of that.

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:13.826
I was like, when I read this,
is it going to --

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:17.530
is it going to be
trite or shallow?

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.365
You know,
because it is optimistic.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:23.035
And it's about being hopeful
despite having hard times.

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:25.838
And it actually really held up.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:30.009
It made it seem -- It actually
made me wake up to the idea

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:33.079
that hope is something
I really cherish

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:36.716
and always want
and work hard on keeping.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:40.152
And it's easy when things
are going pretty good.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:41.554
It's always -- To be like,

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:42.821
"Yeah, I'm pretty hopeful,
things are going to work out."

00:03:42.821 --> 00:03:45.391
But when things are heavy,
to be hopeful,

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:47.360
that's the real challenge
of being hopeful.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.095
And that's where the book
kind of really

00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:52.498
just took on
a little bit more resonance.

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:55.969
-You recognized
coughing as a problem

00:03:55.969 --> 00:03:58.403
before we were
in the middle of this.

00:03:58.403 --> 00:04:01.941
You believe this nation
has an epidemic of coughing.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.409
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:04.977
In the book, I do call out

00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:06.945
a couple of moments
that are like, "Oh."

00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:09.982
I was flying a lot, you know,
as a touring comedian.

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:12.084
And I say in the book --
I'm like, "Look.

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:14.720
I know the scientists are
working on some big things.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.656
We've got cancer.
We've got some heart disease.

00:04:17.656 --> 00:04:20.192
But there's a coughing
epidemic going on.

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:22.528
Every flight I'm on,
people are hacking up a lung.

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:24.330
Please, let's put
some money towards that."

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.732
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

